<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Pashalik of Scutari</h1><p class="st-meta">1757 CE – 1831 CE · capital Shkodër</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Semi-independent Ottoman province of the Bushati family in northern Albania, whose pashas waged their own wars and defied the sultan for decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-pasha-bushati">Mehmed Pasha Bushati</a>, from a powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> family, was appointed governor of the sanjak of Scutari in 1757 and made the post hereditary in practice. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushati-family">Bushatis</a> used the weakness of central Ottoman government in the later 18th century to build an autonomous domain over northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The pashas ruled through Albanian clan chiefs and their own armies, collecting taxes and trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a>. Mehmed built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lead-mosque-shkoder">Lead Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> in 1773. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mahmud-pasha-bushati">Kara Mahmud Pasha Bushati</a> expanded the domain, negotiated directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and fought the Ottoman government, but was killed fighting the Montenegrins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-krusi">Krusi</a> in 1796. Catholic and Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> coexisted.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-pasha-bushati">Mustafa Pasha Bushati</a>, the last ruler, joined Bosnian notables against the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a>. In 1831 the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resid-mehmed-pasha">Reşid Mehmed Pasha</a> defeated him and forced his surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozafa-castle">Rozafa</a> castle, restoring direct Ottoman rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pashalik-of-scutari.jpg" alt="Pashalik of Scutari" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Turtle-bienhoa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mahmut_Pasha_Bushatli_-_1796.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1757 CE – 1831 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shkodër</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mehmed Pasha Bushati, Kara Mahmud Pasha Bushati, Mustafa Pasha Bushati</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Albania, Montenegro, Kosovo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bushati Pashalik, Pashalik of Shkodër</dd></dl></aside></div>

